NEW = {
    'documentation/HANDOFF_L385_opening_views_20261019.md': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Session record of the orrery\'s opening views (L-385), the first of the Sun\'s drawings (L-412), and the design of the Sun\'s two fades (L-410 with L-131); three patches, with Tony\'s run order. -->\n# Handoff: the opening views (L-385), and the Sun\'s fades designed\n\nBuilt on orrery 6e0cfacd0b6aa42cb09b0ca765be94e68aab6f70 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery ("L455 earth\nnumbers") and gallery 4fc320fa6d2e0e10d9a50b8b69006cbff96a1364 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io ("daily\nrun 10-19-26"). Nothing pushed by this session; Tony pushes. The\nwebsite is not touched.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nType: BUILD, with a DESIGN talk (zero code) in its second half.\nWritten October 19, 2026 by Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\nFollows `HANDOFF_L455_earth_numbers_20261018.md`, section 7.\nCompanions: `SOURCES_L410_sun_corona_dust_20261019.md`, the design\ntalk\'s sources and rulings; `WORDS_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md`, the\nwords a visitor will read, revision 1, for your review;\n`prompts/RELAY_L410_gemini_sun_fades_20261019.md`, the cross-check\nprompt for Gemini 4, and `REVIEW_L410_gemini_worksheet_20261019.md`,\nits return reviewed.\n\n## 1. Your run order (every Tony-action of this session, in one list)\n\nEach patch checks the files it edits and refuses rather than guess.\n\n1. **(done)** Orrery folder: `patch_L385_1_opening_views_20261019.py`,\n   run this morning; your look: "perfect" for the Sun and for Earth.\n2. **(do)** Orrery folder: run `patch_L385_2_every_view_20261019.py`.\n3. **(look)** Mode 5 in the orrery, Auto scale: the Sun centered with\n   only the photosphere ticked and Earth plotted. Expect Earth\'s orbit\n   in the frame, about 1.22 AU. Then the same as an animation; then\n   Earth centered with the crust ticked and the Moon plotted (the\n   Moon\'s orbit in the frame).\n4. **(do)** Orrery folder: run `patch_L385_3_session_close_20261019.py`,\n   the records.\n5. **(do)** Orrery maintenance run (dashboard). Expect 31 of 31 gating\n   checkers passed, the gate line "0 TIER-1", "Orbit markers" 12 of\n   12, and "Skill headers" OK. The whole tree reads 284, "dev tools 4":\n   patches 1 and 2 are scanned while they sit in the folder, and it\n   reads 280 once they are moved in step 6. It rewrites the Skill\n   Manifest to orrery-coding-conventions 1.11 and\n   provenance-cross-check 1.1.\n6. **(do)** Move the three patch scripts into `documentation/`; commit;\n   push.\n7. **(do)** Reinstall two skills in Settings > Skills:\n   orrery-coding-conventions (1.11) and provenance-cross-check (1.1).\n8. **(done)** Gemini\'s worksheet came back the same evening; save it as\n   `documentation/worksheets/worksheet_gemini4_standard_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md`\n   and commit it with the rest. Reviewed in\n   `REVIEW_L410_gemini_worksheet_20261019.md`.\n9. **(decide)** The fades\' words, revision 2\n   (`WORDS_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md`): answer in place, before the\n   build, including section 3: forward scattering stays out, the\n   dust\'s origin now has a source, and a temperature is offered.\n\n## 2. What was built\n\n**The rule.** Every view, still or animated, opens on the farthest\nthing drawn, plus 20 percent. A shell counts by its radius, or for a\ncustom shell (low orbit, a magnetosphere, a belt) by the reach of\nwhat it draws. An orbit counts by the aphelion of a plotted object\'s\ndrawn ellipse; a moon only when it is plotted. The line toward the Sun\ndoes not count unless nothing else is drawn. The center body\'s\nrotation axis and dipole cone end just past its farthest shell, never\nlonger than their set length.\n\n**Patch 1** (planet_visualization.py, planet_visualization_utilities.py,\npalomas_orrery.py). The Auto view of a center body with shells: the\nfarthest shell times 1.2. Low orbit\'s fallback of 100 Earth radii,\ndoubled, is gone; the Sun\'s asteroid belts count as shells; the axis\nand cone builders take a length limit.\n\n**Patch 2** (palomas_orrery.py, test_orbit_markers.py). One margin at\nevery scale, where it was 50, 30, 20 or 10 percent. The still plot\ntakes the larger of the shells and the orbits, as the animation does.\nWith nothing orbiting, the shells alone size the view, not the 1 AU\ndefault. A moon counts only when plotted: until now every child did,\nwhich would have opened Earth\'s crust on the Moon\'s orbit. The\nanimation\'s Sun arrow is clamped to the view it opens on. The orbit\nmarkers check expects the one margin.\n\n**Patch 3** (the records). orrery-coding-conventions 1.11, The Opening\nView, with its read plan; provenance-cross-check 1.1, The Slice Relay,\nthe method of the Gemini prompt, on your request; protocol v3.104; the ledger (L-385, L-410,\nL-131); Where We Are; this handoff; the sources record.\n\nBefore and after, measured on the live dispatch:\n\n| View | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sun, photosphere only | 62 solar radii | 1.2 solar radii |\n| Earth, crust only | 3.7 Earth radii | 1.2 Earth radii |\n| Earth, low orbit only | 200 Earth radii | 1.6 Earth radii |\n| Sun, photosphere, Earth plotted | 0.29 AU, Earth outside | 1.22 AU |\n| Animated Sun, photosphere only | 1 AU | 1.2 solar radii |\n\n## 3. Tony\'s rulings this session\n\n- The axis: shorten it or let it run off the view? "Confirmed as\n  recommended": shortened for the center body, never lengthened.\n- The Sun and Earth after patch 1, in his run record\n  (`WHERE_WE_ARE_10-18-26_1326_run_record.md`, session of 10-19): "perfect.\n  the rotation axis is within the cube", and "perfect. the sun\n  direction, rotation axis and magnetic cone are inside the cube."\n- The other views: "confirmed as recommended": the farthest thing\n  drawn, shell or orbit, plus 20 percent, still and animated alike.\n- The Sun\'s fades, six rulings: the corona and the dust are two\n  drawings, because they are different physics (his correction of\n  Claude\'s one-cloud proposal); a dust dot\'s place is density and its\n  brightness the sunlight it receives; that light falls with the\n  square of distance, not the cube; the inner and outer corona become\n  one plasma fade out to the Alfven surface; the dust\'s dots stop at\n  5.2 AU; the corona\'s dots follow the same place-and-brightness rule. Each is quoted in `SOURCES_L410_sun_corona_dust_20261019.md`\n  and on L-410 and L-131.\n\n## 4. How it was checked\n\n- Both code patches run in order on a clean copy at 6e0cfacd: byte for\n  byte the tested files; on Windows line endings too; a second run\n  refuses.\n- The orrery maintenance run on the patched copy: 31 of 31 gating, gate\n  path 0 Tier-1, whole tree 280, no file\'s count rose. A first try had\n  raised planet_visualization.py\'s count by 4, from two named rendering\n  settings and numbers typed in docstrings; the settings now sit inside\n  the functions that use them and the docstrings say it in words.\n- The pre-test: the orrery compiles and opens its window headless; the\n  real module run headless, its live dispatch measured for every view\n  in the table above.\n- Tony\'s saved plots after patch 1: the Sun\'s view 0.00558 AU, Earth\'s\n  1.2 Earth radii.\n- Not checked here: an animation end to end, which fetches from JPL;\n  Tony\'s look at step 3 covers it.\n\n## 5. Skill versions and what travels\n\nThis session\'s loaded copies read orrery-coding-conventions 1.10,\nprovenance-cross-check 1.0, provenance-discipline 2.31 (discharging\nv3.103\'s obligation),\nsafe-file-editing 1.13, agentic-pre-test 1.3 and\nledger-and-session-records 1.21, as the manifest expected.\norrery-coding-conventions went to 1.11 and provenance-cross-check to\n1.1 in patch 3; the next session confirms its loaded copies read those\nbefore work that fires them.\n\n## 6. What is left, and the next session\n\n- L-385 closes on your look at step 3.\n- The Sun\'s two fades are designed and next: the plasma corona (L-410)\n  and the dust cloud (L-131). Open points for the build are listed at\n  the end of the sources record. The words are drafted as revision 1\n  (`WORDS_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md`); your answers there come before\n  the build, including its two questions in section 3.\n- After them, in the Sun\'s slice: the streamer belt\'s seven numbers\n  set by eye, and L-241\'s Hills wording re-read against the fades.\n- Carried, not chased: the dispatch still measures the Sun direction\n  arrow inside the shells\' view when orbits widen the final view, so\n  the arrow can read short there. It stays inside the view; noted for\n  your eye.\n\nStarting prompt for the next session:\n\n```\nBuild session: the Sun\'s two fades (L-410 with L-131), as designed on\nOctober 19: the plasma corona as one fade out to the Alfven surface,\nand the dust cloud as stored dots, place for density and brightness for\nsunlight (the corona\'s dots too), stopping at 5.2 AU. The words are\ndocumentation/WORDS_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md with my answers in it.\nPull both repositories and say the SHAs;\nthe design is documentation/SOURCES_L410_sun_corona_dust_20261019.md\nand the handoff documentation/HANDOFF_L385_opening_views_20261019.md.\nConfirm your loaded skills against the manifest before work that fires\nthem. Every word a visitor reads comes to me as a list first. My eye is\nthe gate. One question per message, plain speech.\n```\n\nSession written October 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n',
    'documentation/SOURCES_L410_sun_corona_dust_20261019.md': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Sources found for the Sun\'s two fades (L-410 with L-131): the plasma corona and the zodiacal dust cloud. Each value with what was opened, where in it, and what it says. Read 2026-10-19 by Claude Opus 5.5. -->\n# Sources: the Sun\'s corona and dust cloud (L-410 with L-131)\n\nBuilt on orrery 6e0cfacd0b6aa42cb09b0ca765be94e68aab6f70 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and gallery\n4fc320fa6d2e0e10d9a50b8b69006cbff96a1364 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nType: DESIGN (zero code). Written October 19, 2026 by Anthropic\'s\nClaude Opus 5.5, during the design talk. Nothing here is in\nconstants_new.py yet; the fades\' build writes the rows, and each\nrow\'s wording comes to Tony as a list first.\n\nEvery source below was opened in this session, from an open copy.\n"Read" means the page was fetched and the figure found at the place\nnamed. Where a figure is worked out from what a source prints, the\nline says so.\n\n## Tony\'s rulings so far (2026-10-19)\n\n- The corona and the dust cloud are two drawings, because they are\n  different physics: the corona is plasma whose glow is sunlight\n  scattered by free electrons (the K-corona); the F-corona is sunlight\n  scattered by dust, the innermost part of the zodiacal light. "Yes."\n- In the dust drawing, a dot\'s place shows the dust\'s density, and its\n  brightness shows the sunlight it receives, which falls with the\n  square of distance from the Sun. "Confirmed as recommend."\n- The square, not the cube: "Confirmed." (The cube belongs to Earth\'s\n  hydrogen fall-off beyond 15 Earth radii, a dipole\'s field and tidal\n  pull.)\n- The inner corona (3 solar radii) and outer corona (50 solar radii)\n  spheres become ONE plasma fade, following the measured electron\n  density, from the Sun\'s surface out to the Alfven surface, which\n  stays drawn as it is. "Confirmed as recommended."\n- The dust cloud\'s dots stop at 5.2 AU, where Kelsall et al.\'s model\n  stops its line-of-sight sums; a declared drawing choice, and the\n  hover says the cloud has no edge and the drawing stops where that\n  model stops. "Confirmed."\n- The corona\'s dots follow the same rule as the dust: place for the\n  electron density, brightness for the sunlight each dot receives, since\n  the corona\'s glow is also scattered sunlight. "Yes confirmed."\n\n## Found later the same day (open points closed)\n\nMann, Warmuth, Vocks and Rouillard, "A heliospheric density and\nmagnetic field model", A&A 679, A64 (2023), open access (CC BY 4.0).\nRead 2026-10-19.\n- Eq. 7, the Leblanc et al. (1998) model: N_e(r) = 3.3e5 / R^2 +\n  4.1e6 / R^4 + 8.0e7 / R^6 electrons per cubic centimetre, R = r in\n  solar radii; for the region between the corona and Earth\'s orbit in\n  the ecliptic plane; 7.14 per cubic centimetre at 1 AU.\n- The article\'s own model, eq. 11 and Table 8, fitted over 1 to 250\n  solar radii from Parker\'s wind equation.\n- Open copy: https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2023/11/aa45050-22/aa45050-22.html\n- So the corona\'s fade has an open source covering the whole drawn\n  range, surface to the Alfven surface, in one closed form. Baumbach\'s\n  formula (Lemaire, above) holds only to 3 solar radii. Recommended\n  for the build, by method (one open model across the drawn range, the\n  simplest form): Leblanc et al. as quoted by Mann et al. The original\n  1998 paper (Sol. Phys. 183, 165) is not open; Saito et al. (1977)\n  was not found open either, so neither is cited at the source.\n\nHoward, Vourlidas, Bothmer, Colaninno, DeForest, Gallagher, Hall,\nHess, Higginson, Korendyke, Kouloumvakos, Lamy, Liewer, Linker,\nLinton, Penteado, Plunkett, Poirier, Raouafi, Rich, Rochus, Rouillard,\nSocker, Stenborg, Thernisien and Viall, Nature 576, 232-236 (2019).\nThe author list as printed on its RePEc record, read 2026-10-19:\nhttps://ideas.repec.org/a/nat/nature/v576y2019i7786d10.1038_s41586-019-1807-x.html\n\nThe dust\'s midplane, by method: the drawing follows Kelsall et al.\'s\nfit throughout (node 77.7 degrees, tilt 2.03), because its shape\nparameters were fitted together and mixing in another paper\'s node\nwould draw a cloud neither paper measured. Lamy et al.\'s warped inner\nplane is then said in words in the panel, as a thing the drawing does\nnot show.\n\n## Gemini\'s worksheet, the same evening\n\nReviewed in `REVIEW_L410_gemini_worksheet_20261019.md`. What it\nchanges here, each re-opened before being written down:\n- The Leblanc model holds only beyond about 1.8 solar radii: Zic,\n  Vrsnak and Temmer, "Heliospheric Propagation of Coronal Mass\n  Ejections: Drag-Based Model Fitting", arXiv 1506.08582, which says\n  the expression is valid for R > 1.8. So, by the one-model method\n  above, the corona\'s fade follows Mann et al. (2023)\'s own model, which\n  covers 1 to 250 solar radii (eq. 11, Table 8); Leblanc is a\n  cross-check beyond 1.8. This replaces the recommendation above.\n- Where the dust comes from: Nesvorny, Jenniskens, Levison, Bottke and\n  Vokrouhlicky, arXiv 0909.4322 (2010), abstract: 85-95% of the\n  mid-infrared emission from Jupiter-family comets, under 10% from\n  long-period comets, asteroidal dust under 10%, about 85% of the mass\n  reaching Earth. Open: https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4322\n- Kelsall et al. give a reason for 5.2 AU: it roughly corresponds to\n  the orbit of Jupiter (sec. 4.2, Gemini\'s quote; our read of sec. 4.2\n  had reached the cutoff and not the reason).\n- Forward scattering is still unsourced; the words keep it out.\n\n## Still to settle, at the build (method, not rulings)\n\n- The depletion zone as drawn: the dust thins from about 19 solar\n  radii and is gone by about 5 (Lamy et al.), so the corona\'s fade and\n  the dust overlap between them.\n- The outer-corona checkbox\'s new name, and every visitor-facing word,\n  come to Tony as a list before they ship.\n\n## 1. What the two lights are\n\nBoe, Habbal, Downs and Druckmuller, "The Color and Brightness of the\nF-Corona Inferred from the 2019 July 2 Total Solar Eclipse", arXiv\n2103.02113, introduction. Read 2026-10-19.\n- The K-corona is Thomson scattering of photospheric light by free\n  electrons throughout the corona.\n- The F-corona is dust throughout the solar system scattering and/or\n  diffracting photospheric light, often called the part of the\n  zodiacal light at the smallest elongations from the Sun.\n- Open copy: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.02113\n\n## 2. The dust cloud\'s shape and density (the zodiacal cloud)\n\nKelsall, Weiland, Franz, Reach, Arendt, Dwek, Freudenreich, Hauser,\nMoseley, Odegard, Silverberg and Wright, "The COBE Diffuse Infrared\nBackground Experiment Search for the Cosmic Infrared Background: II.\nModel of the Interplanetary Dust Cloud", arXiv astro-ph/9806250\n(ApJ 508, 44, 1998). Read 2026-10-19.\n- Sec. 4.2.1, eq. 6: the smooth cloud\'s density is n0 times R^-alpha\n  times f(zeta), zeta the height over the cloud\'s midplane divided by\n  distance; eq. 7: f(zeta) = exp(-beta g^gamma), g = zeta^2 / 2 mu below\n  mu and zeta - mu/2 above ("widened, modified fan").\n- Table 1, "Density and Geometrical Parameters of the IPD Model",\n  final values with 68% joint uncertainties:\n\n  | Row | Value | Uncertainty |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | alpha, radial power-law exponent | 1.34 | 0.022 |\n  | beta, vertical shape | 4.14 | 0.067 |\n  | gamma, vertical power-law exponent | 0.942 | 0.025 |\n  | mu, widening | 0.189 | 0.014 |\n  | i, inclination of the midplane | 2.03 deg | 0.017 |\n  | Omega, ascending node | 77.7 deg | 0.6 |\n\n  (n0, 1.13e-7 per AU, is a cross-section density at 1 AU, not a\n  number of grains; the drawing does not need it.)\n- Sec. 4.2: the model\'s line-of-sight integration stops at an outer\n  radial cutoff of 5.2 AU. That is the model\'s cutoff, not a measured\n  edge: the cloud thins with no edge (a kind-1 fade, L-410\'s ruling of\n  2026-10-17).\n- Open copy: https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9806250\n\n## 3. The dust close to the Sun (the F-corona and the dust-free zone)\n\nLamy, Gilardy and Llebaria, "Observations of the Solar F-corona from\nSpace", arXiv 2202.11533 (Space Sci. Rev., doi\n10.1007/s11214-022-00918-y). Read 2026-10-19.\n- Abstract, sec. 4.4: the F-corona\'s radiance falls as a power law of\n  elongation between 5 and 50 degrees, exponent -2.33 near the\n  equator and -2.55 over the poles.\n- Sec. 4 and 4.1: the dust density is taken as r^-nu, with the\n  radiance exponent nu + 1; the Helios value of the radiance exponent\n  is about 2.3. Worked out here, not printed: nu is about 1.3,\n  agreeing with Kelsall\'s 1.34.\n- Abstract: the outer border of the dust depletion zone is well\n  constrained at about 19 solar radii, and the zone extends down to\n  about 5 solar radii, the boundary of the dust-free zone.\n- Abstract: the inner cloud\'s symmetry plane is strongly warped; its\n  ascending node is constant at 87.6 degrees. (This differs from\n  Kelsall\'s 77.7, which fits the cloud near 1 AU; to settle at the\n  build which the drawing follows, or neither.)\n- Open copy: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.11533\n\nHoward and others, "Near-Sun observations of an F-corona decrease and\nK-corona fine structure", Nature 576, 232 (2019), doi\n10.1038/s41586-019-1807-x. Abstract read 2026-10-19 (author list not\nread on the page; to be read at the build).\n- Parker Solar Probe\'s imaging on its first two perihelia, at 0.16 to\n  0.25 AU, shows the F-corona\'s intensity decreasing at short\n  elongations, suggestive of the long-sought dust-free zone.\n- Open abstract: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1807-x\n\n## 4. The plasma corona\'s density\n\nLemaire, "Determination of coronal temperatures from electron density\nprofiles", arXiv 1112.3850v2 (2012). Read 2026-10-19. A secondary\nsource: it quotes the two models below, which it did not originate.\n- Eq. 5, attributed to Baumbach: n(r) = 1e8 (2.99 r^-16 + 1.55 r^-6 +\n  0.036 r^-1.5) electrons per cubic centimetre, r in solar radii,\n  stated to be limited in principle to 1.05 to 3 solar radii.\n- Eq. 16, the extended Saito model: n(r) = 1e8 [3.09 r^-16 (1 - 0.5\n  sin Phi) + 1.58 r^-6 (1 - 0.95 sin Phi) + 0.0251 r^-2.5 (1 - sin^0.5\n  Phi)] + n_E (215 / r)^2, Phi the latitude, n_E the density at 1 AU.\n  Its last term carries the density out into the solar wind.\n- Open copy: https://arxiv.org/pdf/1112.3850\n- Still to find: the original papers\' open copies (Baumbach 1937,\n  Saito 1977), so the rows can cite what was opened at the source\n  rather than a quotation of it, and n_E\'s value if the Saito form is\n  used.\n\nSession written October 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n',
    'documentation/WORDS_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | The words a visitor will read for the Sun\'s two fades (L-410 with L-131), revision 1, for Tony\'s review before the build. -->\n# Words: the Sun\'s corona and dust cloud, revision 2\n\nBuilt on orrery 6e0cfacd0b6aa42cb09b0ca765be94e68aab6f70 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and gallery\n4fc320fa6d2e0e10d9a50b8b69006cbff96a1364 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nType: DESIGN (zero code). Written October 19, 2026 by Anthropic\'s\nClaude Opus 5.5, for Tony to read before the build. Sources:\n`SOURCES_L410_sun_corona_dust_20261019.md`. Revision 2, the same\nevening, after Gemini\'s worksheet\n(`REVIEW_L410_gemini_worksheet_20261019.md`): 1d\'s model changes,\n3b now has a source, and a temperature is offered (3c).\n\nHow to read this list:\n- A number in braces, {LIKE_THIS}, prints from a row in\n  constants_new.py, with the row\'s citation. No number is typed.\n- The same words serve both sites: the website\'s name, description,\n  hover, panel text ("about") and drawing note; the orrery\'s checkbox\n  name, hover and checkbox tooltip. The hover is the glance; the\n  panel carries the rest.\n- Distances in the orrery\'s hovers also print in AU, as every orrery\n  distance does.\n- Answer each item in place: "-- yes", "-- change to ...", "-- let\'s\n  discuss".\n\n## The rule both drawings follow (Tony, 2026-10-19)\n\nEach dot\'s place shows how dense the gas or dust is; its brightness\nshows how much sunlight reaches it, which falls with the square of\ndistance from the Sun. "Yes confirmed" for the corona as well as the\ndust.\n\n## 1. The corona (replaces "Inner Corona" and "Extended Corona")\n\n**1a. Name.** Corona\n(orrery checkbox: "-- Corona"; the two old checkboxes go)\n\n**1b. Description** (website, one line).\nThe Sun\'s outer atmosphere: a very thin gas that thins out with height\nuntil it becomes the solar wind.\n\n**1c. Hover.**\nCorona\nThe Sun\'s outer atmosphere, a very thin gas of electrons and ions.\nIt has no edge. It thins out with height, and at the Alfven surface,\nabout {ALFVEN_SURFACE_RADII} solar radii ({ALFVEN_SURFACE_RADII in AU} AU)\nfrom the Sun\'s center, it becomes the solar wind.\nEach dot\'s place shows how dense the gas is; its brightness shows how\nmuch sunlight reaches it.\n\n**1d. Panel text** (website "about"; orrery checkbox tooltip).\nThe corona\'s glow, seen at a total eclipse, is sunlight scattered by\nits free electrons, called the K-corona (Boe and others, 2021). How\ndense the gas is at each height comes from a model of the solar wind\nfrom the Sun\'s surface out past Earth\'s orbit (Mann and others, 2023).\nThe dots follow it: crowded at the surface,\nsparse by the Alfven surface. Farther out a second, fainter light\ntakes over, sunlight scattered by dust, the F-corona; that dust is\ndrawn as its own cloud.\n\n**1e. Drawing note** (website `source_note`; the orrery says the same\nin its tooltip).\nThe dots show the model\'s smooth average, drawn the same in every\ndirection. The real corona is lumpy, shaped by the Sun\'s magnetic\nfield into streamers and holes; the streamer belt is drawn\nseparately. The dots stop at the Alfven surface.\n\n**What goes.** The two old hovers and tooltips, and the website\'s\n"Inner Corona" and "Outer Corona" entries. Their temperatures ("1-3\nmillion K", "one to three million kelvin"), the "~2.3" and "~3 R_sun"\ncrossover, and the "1.93 nm" and "1.159 nm" wavelengths have no row\nbehind them and are not carried. If you want a temperature back, it\nneeds a source and a row first.\n\n## 2. The dust cloud (new)\n\n**2a. Name.** Zodiacal Dust Cloud (F-corona)\n(orrery checkbox: "-- Zodiacal Dust Cloud (F-corona)")\n\n**2b. Description** (website, one line).\nDust spread through the inner solar system in a flattened cloud. Near\nthe Sun its scattered sunlight is the F-corona.\n\n**2c. Hover.**\nZodiacal Dust Cloud\nDust spread through the inner solar system, flattened toward the plane\nthe planets move in. It thins with distance from the Sun and has no\nedge; the drawing stops at {ZODIACAL_DUST_OUTER_AU} AU.\nEach dot\'s place shows how dense the dust is; its brightness shows how\nmuch sunlight reaches it.\nNear the Sun this dust is seen as the F-corona.\n\n**2d. Panel text.**\nThis dust scatters sunlight. Near the Sun that light is the F-corona,\nthe part of the zodiacal light closest to the Sun (Boe and others,\n2021). The cloud\'s shape follows the model fitted to the COBE\nsatellite\'s infrared sky: its density falls with distance from the\nSun to the power {ZODIACAL_DUST_DENSITY_EXPONENT}, and it is flattened\ntoward a middle plane tilted {ZODIACAL_DUST_TILT_DEG} degrees to\nEarth\'s orbit (Kelsall and others, 1998). Close to the Sun the dust\nthins from about {DUST_DEPLETION_OUTER_RADII} solar radii inward and\nis gone by about {DUST_FREE_ZONE_RADII}, the dust-free zone (Lamy and\nothers, 2022); Parker Solar Probe saw the first signs of it (Howard\nand others, 2019). There, the cloud\'s middle plane is also warped\n(Lamy and others, 2022), which the drawing does not show.\n\n**2e. Drawing note.**\nThe cloud has no edge. The dots stop at {ZODIACAL_DUST_OUTER_AU} AU,\nwhere the COBE model stops adding up its light. Only the model\'s\nsmooth cloud is drawn, not the narrow dust bands it also includes.\n\n## 3. Left out on purpose, for you to decide\n\n**3a. Forward scattering.** Dust sends most of the light it scatters\nnearly straight on, which is why the F-corona is brightest just beside\nthe Sun, and it depends on where the viewer stands, so no drawing of\nthe cloud can show it. I would like to say this in the panel, but the\nsources read so far do not state it in words, so it is out until one\ndoes. Keep looking for a source, or leave it out?\n\n**3a, after Gemini.** Still unsourced. Its sources were a paper about\ndust on a telescope\'s own lenses, a paper not re-opened, and a\ncommercial web page; a review re-opened here does not say it either.\nRecommended: leave it out.\n\n**3b. Where the dust comes from: now sourced.** Proposed for the end of\n2d:\nJudged by its infrared glow, most of this dust comes from comets\nwhose orbits Jupiter shapes, the Jupiter-family comets:\n{ZODIACAL_DUST_JFC_LOW_PERCENT} to {ZODIACAL_DUST_JFC_HIGH_PERCENT}\npercent. Asteroids give less than {ZODIACAL_DUST_ASTEROID_MAX_PERCENT}\n(Nesvorny and others, 2010).\nKeep, change, or leave out?\n\n**3c. A temperature for the corona (optional).** NASA\'s "Our Sun:\nFacts" page gives the corona as reaching up to 2 million degrees\nCelsius, which is 2 million kelvin to the figure printed. Proposed for\nthe end of 1d: "The corona is very hot, up to about\n{CORONA_TEMPERATURE_MAX_K} kelvin, far hotter than the surface below\nit (NASA)." The page is re-opened at the build before it becomes a\nrow. Keep it, or leave the temperature out?\n\n## 4. The rows these words need (written at the build)\n\n| Row | Value | Kind | From |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| the corona\'s density | Table 8 | measured | Mann et al. 2023, their own model, 1 to 250 solar radii; Leblanc (eq. 7) only beyond 1.8 |\n| ZODIACAL_DUST_DENSITY_EXPONENT | 1.34 | measured | Kelsall et al. 1998, Table 1 |\n| ZODIACAL_DUST_BETA, _GAMMA, _MU | 4.14, 0.942, 0.189 | measured | same |\n| ZODIACAL_DUST_TILT_DEG, _NODE_DEG | 2.03, 77.7 | measured | same |\n| ZODIACAL_DUST_OUTER_AU | 5.2 | declared | the model\'s cutoff, sec. 4.2 |\n| DUST_DEPLETION_OUTER_RADII | 19 | measured | Lamy et al. 2022, abstract |\n| DUST_FREE_ZONE_RADII | 5 | measured | same |\n| ZODIACAL_DUST_JFC_LOW_PERCENT, _HIGH_PERCENT | 85, 95 | measured | Nesvorny et al. 2010, abstract (3b, if kept) |\n| ZODIACAL_DUST_ASTEROID_MAX_PERCENT | 10 | measured | same |\n| CORONA_TEMPERATURE_MAX_K | 2e6 | measured | NASA, "Our Sun: Facts" (3c, if kept) |\n\nALFVEN_SURFACE_RADII is already a row. The names are proposals; the\nbuild settles them by the store\'s conventions.\n\nSession written October 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n',
    'documentation/prompts/RELAY_L410_gemini_sun_fades_20261019.md': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Relay prompt for Gemini 4: check the sources behind the Sun\'s two fades (L-410 with L-131) and search for the ones still missing. Copy everything below the line into Gemini. -->\n# Relay prompt for Gemini 4 -- the Sun\'s corona and dust cloud (L-410)\n\nWritten 2026-10-19 by Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5 for Tony to carry to\nGemini 4. Copy everything below the line. Ask for the highest effort\ntier Gemini offers: six of the rows are DISCOVERY rows, which are only\nas good as how long the checker keeps looking.\n\n---\n\nBuilt on palomas_orrery 6e0cfacd0b6aa42cb09b0ca765be94e68aab6f70 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and\ntonyquintanilla.github.io 4fc320fa6d2e0e10d9a50b8b69006cbff96a1364 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io. The rows\nbelow are NOT in either repository yet, so every value you need is\nwritten out here.\nVocabulary: v2 (2026-08-13)\n\n## Who you are working for\n\nTony Quintanilla, PE, a retired civil and environmental engineer, builds\nPaloma\'s Orrery, a 3D astronomy visualization (a desktop program and the\nwebsite palomasorrery.com), through conversation with AI partners. He is\nnot a professional programmer or astronomer. Every number a visitor\nreads must come from a source anyone can open. Please write plainly.\n\n## What is being drawn\n\nTwo new drawings of the Sun\'s surroundings, both as clouds of dots:\n\n- The CORONA, the Sun\'s outer atmosphere of hot plasma, as one cloud\n  thinning outward from the Sun\'s surface to the Alfven surface (about\n  19.7 solar radii), where it becomes the solar wind. The dots are\n  placed by a model of the electron density.\n- The ZODIACAL DUST CLOUD, as a separate cloud: dust flattened toward\n  the planets\' plane, thinning with distance, thinning out again close\n  to the Sun (the dust-free zone), and drawn out to 5.2 AU. Near the Sun\n  its scattered sunlight is the F-corona.\n\nIn both, a dot\'s place shows density and its brightness shows the\nsunlight it receives, which falls with the square of distance.\n\n## The rules this job follows\n\nIf you have imported the palomas_orrery repository, read these two\nfiles and name them at the top of your answer:\n\n- skills/provenance-discipline/SKILL.md -- sections The Access Standard\n  and What a Visitor Reads\n- skills/provenance-cross-check/references/worksheets.md -- section\n  Worksheet Types\n\nIf you have not imported it, say so; the rules you need are restated\nhere:\n\n1. **Open sources only.** A source counts only if its text can be read\n   without a paywall: open full text, a free abstract that states the\n   claim, an agency page or document, an arXiv preprint. A source\n   behind a paywall does not count, whatever its authority.\n2. **Name what you opened.** Give the title and authors as printed in\n   the document you actually opened, not a paper it cites.\n3. **Quote it.** Every row needs a short verbatim quote from the source\n   containing the figure or statement, and a locator (section,\n   equation, table, page, or the exact URL). A row without both is\n   recorded as UNVERIFIED whatever its verdict says. If you are\n   recalling rather than reading, say so and leave the row UNVERIFIED.\n   Silence is better than a guess.\n4. **Temperatures are stored in kelvin.** If a source gives degrees\n   Celsius, report the figure exactly as the source prints it; the\n   conversion is ours.\n5. **Prefer an agency or a peer-reviewed paper** (NASA, ESA, an open\n   journal article or arXiv preprint) over an educational or news page.\n   Say which kind each source is.\n\n## Before any row: a pre-flight\n\nOpen this page and quote one sentence from it, so we know your fetching\nworks:\n\n```\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11533\n```\n\n## Your answer\'s header\n\nStart with one line naming your MODEL and your EFFORT TIER, then the\nlist of rule files you read (or "not imported").\n\n## The two kinds of row\n\n[CITATION] -- the row names a source. Open it and confirm it states the\nfigure or statement, at the figure\'s precision. Then, separately, check\nthe value against one other open source of your own choosing,\npreferably an agency or a paper.\n\n[DISCOVERY] -- the claim may have no source at all. Do not assume one\nexists, and do not reconcile it with any other row. Find whether an\nopen source states it. UNSOURCED is a valid and useful answer, but a\nnegative must show its search: list the queries you actually ran and\nthe places you looked, in a SEARCH LOG under the table. A negative with\nno search log is recorded as UNVERIFIED.\n\n## The table\n\nOne row per numbered item below, in this exact layout:\n\n| # | Job | Claim | Code value | Your value | Source unit | Source (URL opened, access word) | Value correct? | Citation correct? | Read by | Quote | Locator | Notes |\n\n- **Read by**: who opened the source -- your model name.\n- **Access word**: one of open full text, abstract, agency page, open\n  document, educational page, news page.\n- Each verdict cell holds EXACTLY ONE token, and the tokens belong to\n  their column:\n\n```\nValue correct?     YES  NO  APPROX  UNVERIFIED\nCitation correct?  YES  NO  PARTIAL  DERIVED  UNSOURCED  UNVERIFIED\n```\n\n  APPROX qualifies a value (right to a stated tolerance; say the\n  tolerance in Notes). PARTIAL qualifies a citation (the source supports\n  part of the claim). UNSOURCED means the named source does not publish\n  the figure at all; NO means it publishes a different one. For a\n  DISCOVERY row, "Citation correct?" answers whether the source you\n  found states the claim.\n\n- Put every URL as a bare address inside a code block, after the table,\n  numbered by row. Links pasted as chips lose their address.\n- Please deliver the whole answer as a downloadable Markdown file named\n  worksheet_gemini4_<effort tier>_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md, as well\n  as in the chat.\n\n## The rows\n\n### Citation rows (a source is named)\n\nC1. The K-corona is sunlight scattered by free electrons in the corona;\nthe F-corona is sunlight scattered or diffracted by dust, and is the\npart of the zodiacal light closest to the Sun. Source named: Boe,\nHabbal, Downs and Druckmuller, "The Color and Brightness of the\nF-Corona Inferred from the 2019 July 2 Total Solar Eclipse", arXiv\n2103.02113, introduction. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.02113\n\nC2. In the COBE zodiacal cloud model, the smooth cloud\'s density falls\nwith distance from the Sun as R to the power -alpha, alpha = 1.34\n(+/- 0.022). Source named: Kelsall et al. (1998), "The COBE Diffuse\nInfrared Background Experiment Search for the Cosmic Infrared\nBackground: II. Model of the Interplanetary Dust Cloud", ApJ 508, 44,\narXiv astro-ph/9806250, eq. 6 and Table 1.\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9806250\n\nC3. The same model\'s vertical shape, eq. 7 (the "widened, modified\nfan"): beta = 4.14 (+/- 0.067), gamma = 0.942 (+/- 0.025), mu = 0.189\n(+/- 0.014). Source named: as C2, Table 1.\n\nC4. The same model\'s midplane: inclination 2.03 degrees (+/- 0.017) to\nthe ecliptic, ascending node 77.7 degrees (+/- 0.6). Source named: as\nC2, Table 1.\n\nC5. The same model integrates along each line of sight out to an outer\nradial cutoff of 5.2 AU from the Sun. Source named: as C2, sec. 4.2.\nPlease also say whether the paper gives a reason for 5.2 AU.\n\nC6. The outer border of the dust depletion zone is at about 19 solar\nradii, and the zone extends down to about 5 solar radii, the boundary\nof the dust-free zone. Source named: Lamy, Gilardy and Llebaria,\n"Observations of the Solar F-corona from Space", Space Sci. Rev.\n(2022), doi 10.1007/s11214-022-00918-y, arXiv 2202.11533, abstract.\nhttps://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.11533\n\nC7. The F-corona\'s radiance falls as a power law of elongation between\n5 and 50 degrees, exponent -2.33 near the equator and -2.55 over the\npoles. Source named: as C6, abstract and sec. 4.4.\n\nC8. The inner zodiacal cloud\'s symmetry plane is strongly warped, and\nits ascending node is constant at 87.6 degrees. Source named: as C6,\nabstract.\n\nC9. Parker Solar Probe\'s imaging on its first two perihelion passes, at\n0.16 to 0.25 AU, showed the F-corona\'s intensity decreasing at short\nelongations, suggestive of the dust-free zone. Source named: Howard et\nal., "Near-Sun observations of an F-corona decrease and K-corona fine\nstructure", Nature 576, 232-236 (2019), doi 10.1038/s41586-019-1807-x,\nabstract.\nhttps://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1807-x\n\nC10. The Leblanc et al. (1998) electron density model: N_e(r) =\n3.3e5 / R^2 + 4.1e6 / R^4 + 8.0e7 / R^6 electrons per cubic\ncentimetre, R in solar radii, for the region between the corona and\nEarth\'s orbit in the ecliptic plane, giving 7.14 per cubic centimetre\nat 1 AU. Source named: Mann, Warmuth, Vocks and Rouillard, "A\nheliospheric density and magnetic field model", A&A 679, A64 (2023),\neq. 7, open access. Please also say from what height above the Sun the\nmodel is stated to apply, if Mann et al. or any open source says.\nhttps://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2023/11/aa45050-22/aa45050-22.html\n\nC11. Baumbach\'s electron density formula: n(r) = 1e8 (2.99 r^-16 +\n1.55 r^-6 + 0.036 r^-1.5) per cubic centimetre, limited in principle to\n1.05 to 3 solar radii. Source named: Lemaire, "Determination of coronal\ntemperatures from electron density profiles", arXiv 1112.3850v2, eq. 5.\nhttps://arxiv.org/pdf/1112.3850\n\n### Discovery rows (no source yet, or a better one wanted)\n\nD1. Forward scattering. Find an open source that states, in words,\nthat interplanetary dust scatters sunlight mostly forward (a strong\nforward peak in its scattering phase function), and that this is why\nthe F-corona or zodiacal light is brightest close to the Sun. Quote\nthe sentence.\n\nD2. Where the zodiacal dust comes from. Find an open source (an agency\npage or a paper) that states the dust\'s origin: comets, asteroid\ncollisions, or both, with the shares if it gives them. Report every\nfigure you find with its source; do not average them.\n\nD3. The dust\'s density exponent near the Sun. Lamy et al. (C6) give the\nradiance exponent as about 2.3 and the relation radiance exponent =\ndensity exponent + 1, from which we worked out a density exponent of\nabout 1.3; it is not printed there. Find an open source that states the\ninner cloud\'s density exponent directly (for example from the Helios\nprobes), with its value and uncertainty.\n\nD4. One electron density model from the Sun\'s surface out. Find an open\nsource that prints, with numbers, a coronal electron density model\nvalid from about 1 solar radius out to at least 20 solar radii, in one\nform. The Saito, Poland and Munro (1977) model is one candidate; report\nits formula only if an open source prints it.\n\nD5. The corona\'s temperature. Find an agency page or open paper that\nstates the corona\'s typical temperature (the project\'s old text said\none to three million kelvin). Report every figure with its source; do\nnot average them.\n\nD6. Where the F-corona becomes brighter than the K-corona. Find an\nopen source that states the distance from the Sun (in solar radii) at\nwhich the dust\'s light overtakes the electrons\' light. The project\'s old\ntext said the K-corona dominates within about 2.3 solar radii and the\nF-corona beyond about 3.\n\n## What not to do\n\n- Do not propose wording for the website.\n- Do not combine rows, or reconcile one row\'s figure with another\'s.\n- Do not fill a cell from memory. If you could not open a source, the\n  cell is UNVERIFIED and Notes says what stopped you.\n',
    'documentation/REVIEW_L410_gemini_worksheet_20261019.md': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | Review of Gemini\'s L-410 source worksheet for the Sun\'s two fades: what it confirmed, what it found, where it held back, what the re-opening caught, and how it did as a checker. -->\n# Review of Gemini\'s worksheet, L-410 (the Sun\'s corona and dust cloud)\n\nBuilt on palomas_orrery 6e0cfacd0b6aa42cb09b0ca765be94e68aab6f70 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and\ntonyquintanilla.github.io 4fc320fa6d2e0e10d9a50b8b69006cbff96a1364 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nWritten 2026-10-19 by Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5. The worksheet is\n`documentation/worksheets/worksheet_gemini4_standard_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md`,\nsaved by Tony. The prompt it answered is\n`documentation/prompts/RELAY_L410_gemini_sun_fades_20261019.md`. This\nis the second slice relay (provenance-cross-check 1.1, The Slice\nRelay).\n\n## In one sentence\n\nA useful leg that read and quoted throughout, found the corona model\'s\none real limit and the dust\'s origin, and named the wrong authors for\ntwo of the papers it opened, which only re-opening them caught.\n\n## Which model answered\n\nIts header reads "Gemini 3.1 Pro (serving as the requested Gemini 4\nauditor)", effort tier "Standard". The prompt asked for Gemini 4 at its\nhighest tier. The worksheet\'s filename says gemini4; its own header\nsays 3.1 Pro at a standard tier, and the header is the record. Whether\nTony\'s session was Gemini 4 is his to say.\n\n## What it confirmed (citation rows), with a quote and a locator\n\n- C2, C3, C4: Kelsall et al. (1998), Table 1, every value and\n  uncertainty, and the second source it chose (Takimoto et al. 2026)\n  writes the same fan model.\n- C5: the 5.2 AU cutoff, and the paper\'s reason, which the prompt\n  asked for: it "roughly corresponds to the orbit of Jupiter".\n- C6, C8: Lamy et al. (2022), the depletion zone from about 19 to\n  about 5 solar radii, and the warped inner plane, node 87.6 degrees.\n- C9: Howard et al. (2019), the decrease seen at 0.16 to 0.25 AU.\n- C11: Baumbach\'s formula in Lemaire, eq. 5, 1.05 to 3 solar radii.\n\n## Where it held back\n\nIt marked PARTIAL, rather than YES, three rows whose full page it could\nnot open: C1 (only Boe et al.\'s abstract), C7 (the 5 to 50 degree range\ninside Lamy et al.\'s large PDF) and C10 (aanda.org blocked; it read Mann\net al. through ResearchGate). All three stand on this session\'s own\nreads of the full texts, recorded in the sources record. By the\nsend-back rule a PARTIAL earns no cross-check leg; none is claimed.\n\n## What it found, and what re-opening it showed\n\nEvery figure below was re-opened here before anything is built on it.\n\n- **C10, the corona model\'s limit (the important find).** The Leblanc\n  et al. (1998) model holds only beyond about 1.8 solar radii. Re-opened\n  and confirmed: Zic, Vrsnak and Temmer, "Heliospheric Propagation of\n  Coronal Mass Ejections: Drag-Based Model Fitting", arXiv 1506.08582,\n  state the expression "is valid for radial distances R > 1.8". So\n  Leblanc cannot draw the corona from the surface. By the method in the\n  sources record (one open model across the drawn range), the corona\'s\n  fade moves to Mann et al. (2023)\'s own model, which covers 1 to 250\n  solar radii (eq. 11, Table 8); Leblanc stays as a cross-check beyond\n  1.8. Gemini named arXiv 1506.08582 as "Reid & Kontar (2015)"; the\n  paper\'s authors as printed are Zic, Vrsnak and Temmer.\n- **D2, where the dust comes from.** Re-opened: Nesvorny, Jenniskens,\n  Levison, Bottke and Vokrouhlicky, "Cometary Origin of the Zodiacal\n  Cloud and Carbonaceous Micrometeorites. Implications for Hot Debris\n  Disks", arXiv 0909.4322, abstract: 85-95% of the observed mid-infrared\n  emission from particles from the Jupiter-family comets, under 10% from\n  long-period comets, asteroidal dust under 10%, and about 85% of the\n  mass reaching Earth from Jupiter-family comets. Usable for the words\n  list\'s item 3b. Gemini\'s author list adds a sixth name, Gounelle, not\n  on the arXiv copy.\n- **D4, one density model from the surface.** Saito (1970)\'s formula\n  is confirmed in Grechnev, Uralov, Chertok, Kuzmenko, Afanasyev,\n  Meshalkina, Kalashnikov and Kubo, arXiv 1104.3374, eq. 2, which states\n  no radial range for it. Gemini named the paper "Afanasyev & Uralov\n  (2011)". Without a stated range it cannot be the one model; Mann et\n  al. is (above). The Saito, Poland and Munro (1977) two-term fit (Cho\n  et al. 2011) was not re-opened; not needed.\n- **D1, forward scattering: still unsourced.** Its second source,\n  arXiv 2605.12979 (Liu et al., "Forward Modeling of Dust-Induced Stray\n  Light in Ground-Based Coronagraphs"), re-opened: it is about dust on\n  a telescope\'s own optics, and never links forward scattering to the\n  F-corona or the zodiacal light. Its first (Mann et al. 2006) was read\n  through ResearchGate and not re-opened; its third is a commercial\n  web page. Lasue, Levasseur-Regourd and Renard (2020), arXiv\n  2005.07480, re-opened here as a further try, does not state it\n  either. The words keep it out.\n- **D3, the inner density exponent.** Helios, 1.3 +/- 0.05 (Leinert et\n  al. 1981, as reported in two open papers), 1.3 +/- 0.2 in Kuchner et\n  al. (NASA NTRS), Clementine 1.45 +/- 0.05. Not re-opened: they agree\n  with Kelsall\'s 1.34, which the drawing uses, so nothing turns on them.\n- **D5, the corona\'s temperature.** NASA Science, "Our Sun: Facts": up\n  to 2 million degrees Celsius; NSO: 1 million kelvin to more than 10\n  million. Leads, not re-opened; a temperature comes back only if Tony\n  wants one, from the NASA page re-opened at the build.\n- **D6, where the dust\'s light overtakes the electrons\'.** Figures from\n  2.3 to 6 solar radii across five sources, by latitude and the solar\n  cycle; the old text\'s 2.3 and 3 match Shopov et al. (2004). A spread,\n  not one figure; leads, not re-opened, and not in the words.\n\n## How it did, against the rules\n\n- Header: model and tier given, rule files "not imported", said\n  plainly. The pre-flight quote was given.\n- Quotes and locators on every row; one verdict token per cell, from\n  the right column.\n- A search log for every discovery row, with the queries run.\n- Held back on pages it could not open, as PARTIAL with the reason.\n- Three attribution errors, all in second or discovery sources, all\n  found only by re-opening: two papers under the wrong authors, one\n  source that does not say what its row uses it for. This is the slice\n  relay\'s re-open step doing its job: a discovery find is a lead until\n  it is opened again.\n\nSession written October 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n',
}


def read(rel):
    with open(os.path.join(ROOT, rel), 'rb') as f:
        raw = f.read()
    return raw.replace(b'\r\n', b'\n').decode('utf-8'), b'\r\n' in raw


def key(rel, text):
    if rel == 'PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS.md':
        i = text.index(ZONE[0])
        j = text.index(ZONE[1], i) + len(ZONE[1])
        text = text[:i] + text[j:]
    return hashlib.md5(text.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()


def apply(rel, text, edits):
    for k, (old, new) in enumerate(reversed(edits), 1):
        n = text.count(old)
        if n != 1:
            raise RuntimeError('ANCHOR FAIL: %s, edit %d from the bottom, found %d '
                               'times: %r' % (rel, k, n, old[:70]))
        text = text.replace(old, new)
    return text


def main():
    out, crlf = {}, {}
    try:
        for rel, spec in FP.items():
            if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(ROOT, rel)):
                raise RuntimeError('ERROR: %s not found. Save this script in the '
                                   'orrery folder.' % rel)
            text, crlf[rel] = read(rel)
            if key(rel, text) != spec['before']:
                raise RuntimeError('ERROR: %s is not the file at %s.' % (rel, SHA))
            text = apply(rel, text, spec['edits'])
            if key(rel, text) != spec['after']:
                raise RuntimeError('ERROR: %s did not come out as tested.' % rel)
            out[rel] = text
        for rel, edits in ANCHOR.items():
            text, crlf[rel] = read(rel)
            out[rel] = apply(rel, text, edits)
        for rel, text in NEW.items():
            path = os.path.join(ROOT, rel)
            if os.path.exists(path):
                have, crlf[rel] = read(rel)
                if have != text:
                    raise RuntimeError('ERROR: %s already exists and differs.' % rel)
            out[rel] = text
        # The ASCII gate covers what this patch writes: whole files for the
        # five it rebuilds and the two it creates, the inserted lines for
        # the ledger and Where We Are, so your own notes are never judged.
        checked = [(r, t) for r, t in out.items() if r not in ANCHOR]
        checked += [(r, new) for r, edits in ANCHOR.items() for _, new in edits]
        for rel, text in checked:
            if any(ord(ch) > 127 for ch in text):
                raise RuntimeError('ERROR: %s would hold non-ASCII text.' % rel)
    except RuntimeError as e:
        print(e)
        print('NOTHING was written.')
        sys.exit(1)
    for rel, text in out.items():
        with open(os.path.join(ROOT, rel), 'w', encoding='utf-8', newline='') as f:
            f.write(text)
        print('ok  %s' % rel)
        if crlf.get(rel):
            print('note: %s was CRLF in the working copy; written LF' % rel)
    print('patch applied (%d files).' % len(out))
    print('Next: the orrery maintenance run; it writes the read plan, the')
    print('manifest and the ledger index. Then reinstall orrery-coding-conventions')
    print('(1.11) and provenance-cross-check (1.1) in Settings > Skills.')
    print('Undo is Discard Changes in GitHub Desktop.')


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
